  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "kind": "bustout",
      "likelihood": "low",
      "reasoning": "Three disputes warrant investigation, but volume_z is near zero and ticket_drift is below one, providing little support for a sales ramp. Non-delivery, settlement extraction and thin buyer accounts are unverified."
    },
    {
      "kind": "category_norm",
      "likelihood": "med",
      "reasoning": "Ordinary customer-service or fulfillment disputes could explain three cases in a small order sample. Category comparisons and dispute reasons are needed to assess this benign explanation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "seasonal_spike",
      "likelihood": "low",
      "reasoning": "The metrics show little evidence of an unusual volume spike. Seasonal demand remains possible, but would not independently explain the disputes."
    },
    {
      "kind": "data_issue",
      "likelihood": "low",
      "reasoning": "No pipeline failure is established. Duplicate dispute records, denominator errors or incomplete refund ingestion could nevertheless distort the alert."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_action": "reserve",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Reconcile the three disputes to unique orders, reasons, dates and status; verify whether they represent chargebacks and confirm the 65-order denominator.",
    "Check promised delivery dates, carrier acceptance and delivery evidence for disputed orders, then sample other recent orders for recurring fulfillment failures.",
    "Reconcile zero reported refunds against merchant and processor records, including pending requests and ingestion completeness.",
    "Review campaign dates and seasonal/category benchmarks; compare affected buyers with other buyers using available account-history and linkage evidence.",
    "Match settlements to disputed and unfulfilled orders to assess whether payouts preceded a broader delivery breakdown."
  ],
  "memo_markdown": "The alert reports three disputes across 65 trailing-30-day orders (4.6%). This warrants prompt review, but does not establish bust-out. Volume_z is 0.068, ticket_drift is 0.817, and new-buyer GMV share is 14.9%; these do not demonstrate a sales ramp or thin buyer accounts. Zero recorded refunds do not establish successful fulfillment.\n\nRecommend a policy-sized payout reserve, with the percentage and release decision owned by a human reviewer. Reconcile dispute records and the order denominator, inspect fulfillment evidence, verify refund completeness, and review campaign timing and category comparisons.\n\nRepeated overdue, undelivered orders combined with settlement extraction and suspicious buyer patterns would strengthen the bust-out hypothesis and support considering a settlement pause. Verified timely delivery, isolated explained disputes, or corrected records that remove the breach would weaken the alert and support releasing the reserve and monitoring. Campaign evidence alone would not resolve delivery complaints. Offboarding is unsupported without corroborated bust-out evidence."
}
